WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.340
- [Narrator] Your breakfast
may be getting more expensive.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.540
Across the board, Americans
are paying more for food.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.380
Since last year, the cost of
bacon has gone up by over 10%,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.590
6.2% for fresh fruit and 0.2% for cereal.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:16.420
It may not seem like a
lot, but all of these

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.530
little increases add up,
and it's not just food,

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.180
it's goods and services
across the economy.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.100
After years of low inflation,
that hovered around 2%

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.280
the consumer price index, the
benchmark measure of inflation

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.970
in the US, jumped 4.2% in
April from the year before.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.710
These increases are often
felt at the grocery store,

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.146
which is why it's affecting
the price of your breakfast.

00:00:38.146 --> 00:00:41.610
This has created a lot of concern
over whether higher prices

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.630
are a temporary blip, or here to stay.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.780
- During this time of reopening,

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.870
we are likely to see some
upward pressure on prices.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.453
- [Narrator] So here's why

00:00:51.453 --> 00:00:52.420
you're paying more for your breakfast

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.890
and whether the current price jumps

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.283
are a bellwether of inflation to come.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.490
A major factor for increased
food prices as corn,

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:04.560
which has soared roughly 50% in 2021.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.040
Even if you don't have
cornflakes for breakfast,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.420
many foods have ingredients
derived from the grain

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.130
or come from animals who eat it.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.080
Corn makes up over 96% of all
animal feed grain production

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.190
in the US, and around 1/5 of the US crop

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.220
is exported to other countries.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.030
Like China, right now the country
is on a corn buying binge.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.440
Farmers are racing to
fatten millions of hogs

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.060
to replace the pigs it had to kill

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.510
during the 2020 outbreak
of African swine fever.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:35.770
It's expected to import about
four times more corn in 2021,

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.540
mostly from US farmers.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.850
On top of that, drought
in corn producing regions

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.520
in south America have led
to disappointing crop yields

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.160
and logistical problems.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.460
In Argentina, multiple years of drought

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.880
have made the Parana River
too shallow for boats

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.930
fully loaded with grain to pass
from the country's interior

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.380
to Atlantic shipping lanes.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.410
Simply put, the higher
demand for feed corn

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.800
from China and smaller
supply from South America

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.963
means more expensive sausage and bacon.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.490
But that's not all that corn is doing

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.460
to raise the price for your breakfast.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.140
It's also making it more expensive

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.350
to ship food across the country.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.280
About 10% of the gasoline
consumed in the United States

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.380
is ethanol, which is made from corn.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.930
So the rise in price for
the grain has contributed

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.510
to higher gas prices, but
also finding truck drivers

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:23.020
to move the food has been difficult.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.180
Shipping demand for
truck freight has risen

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.290
as the economy has emerged from lockdowns.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.400
To lure new recruits, trucking
companies have raised pay,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.890
further driving up the price of shipping.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.530
Kellogg's, maker of cereal brands

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.160
like Frosted Flakes, has said
that higher shipping costs

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.830
have contributed to it raising prices,

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.090
and those shipping problems

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.310
haven't stopped at the water's edge.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.190
Ocean Freight has made it
more expensive to ship sugar,

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.213
whose price has risen over 3%.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.310
The global supply chain
slowdown last year,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.910
as well as the closing of
the Suez Canal in March,

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.430
have backed up shipping for months.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.290
But economists say food prices
are not the best indicator

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.430
for tracking inflation
across the entire economy.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.120
There are a few reasons for that.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.690
According to senior Wall Street Journal

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.750
economics writer, Jon Hilsenrath.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.220
- It's a very goods oriented
sector of the economy

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.880
as opposed to a labor oriented
sector of the economy,

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.200
like a service sector where labor costs

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.270
really drive the cost of the product.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:24.390
- [Narrator] A more telling indicator

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.810
for broader inflation
might be what you're paying

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.559
for that same breakfast at a restaurant.

00:03:28.559 --> 00:03:33.060
While overall food prices have
jumped 2.4% from a year ago,

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.966
restaurant meals have gone up 3.8%.

00:03:35.966 --> 00:03:38.280
One reason is restaurants
just have more expenses.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.630
For example, takeout
trays became more costly

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.350
after a February winter storm

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.850
knocked out plastics production in Texas.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.960
And another reason is the
increased cost of labor.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.110
Rising vaccination rates, easing lockdowns

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:53.150
and federal stimulus aid have
boosted consumer spending.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:55.320
Yet employers, including
restaurant owners,

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.220
are struggling to find workers.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:00.760
As the economy has
rebounded, so have wages,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.063
but participation in the
labor force has flattened.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.280
- Some people moved out of dense cities

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.510
that were seen as being unsafe
because of the pandemic.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.420
And so as restaurants reopen,

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.960
it might be a geographic problem,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.600
that there just might not
be people in the place.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.070
And then we also have to think
about the policy effects.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.320
So the government has
thrown a lot of money

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.210
at supporting workers who
were affected by the pandemic.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.650
And some workers at this
point might just be saying,

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.210
while they're still getting
these unemployment benefits

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.700
might be saying, you know what?

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.750
I don't have to rush back to work.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:36.130
- [Narrator] Fast food
restaurants have started

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:37.900
to offer perks like signing bonuses

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.330
and free college tuition in a
bid to lower hourly workers.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:44.420
All this adds up to
pricier meals for diners.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.580
- I think most people would agree

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.838
that we wanna see wages rising.

00:04:49.838 --> 00:04:53.570
Particularly for lower
skill, low-income workers,

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.000
when wages are rising, that
means they're better off.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.200
That means they're bringing
home bigger paychecks.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.280
What we don't want to see is
wage increases paired together

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.370
with inflation increases that
start feeding on each other

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.510
and leave workers actually
worse off and not better off.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:09.343
- [Narrator] This effect,

00:05:09.343 --> 00:05:11.010
known as a wage price spiral,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.210
contributed to the high
inflation of the 1970s,

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.570
but will history repeat itself?

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.750
The federal reserve for its part has said

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.130
prices will correct as supply
and demand forces even out.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.160
- These one-time increases in prices

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.060
are likely to have only
transitory effects on inflation.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.730
- [Narrator] For now,
indicators that measure

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:33.170
expectations of inflation,
like treasury bond yields,

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:36.050
remain low, suggesting
people expect inflation

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.970
to be modest, at least in the near term.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.060
So for now, consumers will have to adjust

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:44.120
to some rising prices for their
breakfast, whether at home

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.670
or at the diner, just don't
forget to include the tip.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.083
(gentle music)

